1
00:00:01,150 --> 00:00:02,630
That sensor can't see him.

2
00:00:02,710 --> 00:00:04,955
It's pointed at a plain, blank wall.

3
00:00:05,035 --> 00:00:09,275
Yet researchers have used light bouncing
off a wall to track someone hidden

4
00:00:09,355 --> 00:00:10,355
around a corner.

5
00:00:10,435 --> 00:00:12,555
That dot is their position estimate.

6
00:00:12,635 --> 00:00:14,095
Not a photograph.

7
00:00:14,473 --> 00:00:18,500
The trick is timing: light that
reaches him takes the long way round,

8
00:00:18,580 --> 00:00:19,920
so it comes back later.

9
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,070
A few billionths of a second later.

10
00:00:22,150 --> 00:00:24,070
This takes a time-of-flight sensor,

11
00:00:24,150 --> 00:00:26,715
a camera that clocks its
own light's round trip.

12
00:00:26,795 --> 00:00:30,535
So how do you turn a tiny
delay into a location?

13
00:00:30,745 --> 00:00:34,338
First, a puzzle: why does
a plain wall work at all?

14
00:00:34,418 --> 00:00:37,495
Put a mirror here, and our
friend is simply visible.

15
00:00:37,575 --> 00:00:40,055
A painted wall is rough up close.

16
00:00:40,135 --> 00:00:42,015
It throws light every which way,

17
00:00:42,095 --> 00:00:44,295
so each spot acts less like a mirror,

18
00:00:44,375 --> 00:00:45,935
more like a tiny lamp.

19
00:00:46,015 --> 00:00:48,952
Everything coming back
is a blend of many paths.

20
00:00:49,032 --> 00:00:51,055
What survives is timing.

21
00:00:51,135 --> 00:00:53,615
Nearly everything coming
back bounced once,

22
00:00:53,695 --> 00:00:54,520
off the wall.

23
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:56,680
Our friend's echo bounced three times,

24
00:00:56,760 --> 00:00:57,650
so it's tiny.

25
00:00:57,730 --> 00:00:59,930
This is real data from the same team,

26
00:01:00,010 --> 00:01:02,450
with a different sensor and hidden object:

27
00:01:02,530 --> 00:01:06,290
the wall's big echo, then,
a few nanoseconds later,

28
00:01:06,370 --> 00:01:08,503
a bump you have to zoom in to see.

29
00:01:08,583 --> 00:01:11,570
In this capture, hundreds of times weaker.

30
00:01:11,650 --> 00:01:13,300
That bump is the clue.

31
00:01:13,380 --> 00:01:16,470
Its timing says how much
farther the light travelled.

32
00:01:16,550 --> 00:01:17,910
Farther from where?

33
00:01:17,990 --> 00:01:22,155
Simplify it: the sensor flashes and
listens at one spot on the wall.

34
00:01:22,235 --> 00:01:25,515
Light covers about thirty
centimetres every nanosecond.

35
00:01:25,595 --> 00:01:28,075
But the detour is there and back,

36
00:01:28,155 --> 00:01:32,275
so each extra nanosecond puts him only
about fifteen centimetres farther from

37
00:01:32,355 --> 00:01:33,230
the wall spot.

38
00:01:33,310 --> 00:01:36,905
Measure the extra delay, and you
know how far he is from that spot.

39
00:01:36,985 --> 00:01:38,265
Not which direction.

40
00:01:38,345 --> 00:01:39,320
Just how far.

41
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:41,200
He could be anywhere on this arc,

42
00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:43,290
all the same distance from that spot.

43
00:01:43,370 --> 00:01:45,210
Now listen at a second spot.

44
00:01:45,290 --> 00:01:47,375
Another delay, another arc.

45
00:01:47,475 --> 00:01:51,335
On this side of the wall,
they cross in just one place.

46
00:01:51,415 --> 00:01:53,335
Measured timings are a bit fuzzy,

47
00:01:53,415 --> 00:01:55,295
so each arc is really a band,

48
00:01:55,375 --> 00:01:57,500
and the bands overlap in a small patch.

49
00:01:57,580 --> 00:02:01,100
These two spots are close, so
the patch is long and blurry.

50
00:02:01,180 --> 00:02:02,920
Spread them out, and it shrinks.

51
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:06,360
In practice, a computer
weighs many spots at once,

52
00:02:06,440 --> 00:02:09,810
trying positions and keeping those
whose predicted echoes match.

53
00:02:09,890 --> 00:02:11,570
It also leans on assumptions,

54
00:02:11,650 --> 00:02:13,630
like what kind of object it's after.

55
00:02:13,710 --> 00:02:16,363
That's why the answer
is a likely location,

56
00:02:16,443 --> 00:02:17,470
or a rough shape.

57
00:02:17,550 --> 00:02:18,860
And here's a real one.

58
00:02:18,940 --> 00:02:20,700
Moved through known positions,

59
00:02:20,780 --> 00:02:25,240
the sensor behind that faint bump
rebuilt the rough outline of a hidden U.

60
00:02:25,830 --> 00:02:30,950
In 2012, an MIT team rebuilt the
rough 3D shape of a hidden mannequin.

61
00:02:31,030 --> 00:02:34,545
By 2021, others had live
video around corners.

62
00:02:34,625 --> 00:02:36,705
But those ran on research equipment.

63
00:02:36,785 --> 00:02:39,970
One team had even tracked hidden
objects with a cheap sensor.

64
00:02:40,050 --> 00:02:43,010
Then, in a study published in 2026,

65
00:02:43,090 --> 00:02:47,170
a team from MIT and Dartmouth tried
the small time-of-flight sensors,

66
00:02:47,250 --> 00:02:50,300
often called LiDAR, found
in phones and gadgets.

67
00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:52,300
Don't expect your phone to do this yet:

68
00:02:52,380 --> 00:02:54,985
phone makers often keep
the raw data private.

69
00:02:55,065 --> 00:02:58,785
Their new idea: a model that
keeps track of what moved,

70
00:02:58,865 --> 00:03:01,350
so many weak frames can be combined.

71
00:03:01,430 --> 00:03:04,090
Why would a smaller,
cheaper sensor need that?

72
00:03:04,170 --> 00:03:06,450
Weak lasers mean fainter echoes.

73
00:03:06,530 --> 00:03:09,730
The team's smartphone-grade
device had about a hundred pixels:

74
00:03:09,810 --> 00:03:11,490
a hundred listening spots.

75
00:03:11,570 --> 00:03:13,915
And if you hold one in
your hand, it jiggles.

76
00:03:13,995 --> 00:03:17,195
Their fix borrows night mode's
trick from phone cameras:

77
00:03:17,275 --> 00:03:20,927
stack many quick, dim frames
into one better estimate.

78
00:03:21,007 --> 00:03:23,820
The catch: things move between frames.

79
00:03:23,900 --> 00:03:26,600
He steps, and his echo shifts in time.

80
00:03:26,780 --> 00:03:30,365
The sensor jiggles, and its
listening spots land somewhere new.

81
00:03:30,445 --> 00:03:34,905
Just add them up, and echoes from
different places blur into one smear,

82
00:03:34,985 --> 00:03:37,265
like a long exposure of someone walking.

83
00:03:37,345 --> 00:03:40,095
So their model solves for
one unknown at a time.

84
00:03:40,175 --> 00:03:43,935
To build a shape, hold the object
still and step the sensor through known

85
00:03:44,015 --> 00:03:46,655
positions, spreading
out its listening spots.

86
00:03:46,735 --> 00:03:47,975
That's how the U was made.

87
00:03:48,055 --> 00:03:50,495
To follow a person, hold the sensor still,

88
00:03:50,575 --> 00:03:53,335
and let each guess drift
a little between frames,

89
00:03:53,415 --> 00:03:55,255
keeping the ones that still match.

90
00:03:55,335 --> 00:03:58,195
The estimate keeps up instead of smearing.

91
00:03:58,835 --> 00:04:02,255
So what can it actually
do, and where does it fail?

92
00:04:02,335 --> 00:04:06,455
Take our opening clip: an off-the-shelf
kit the authors put at under a hundred

93
00:04:06,535 --> 00:04:10,340
dollars, held still while a
person walked behind a partition.

94
00:04:10,420 --> 00:04:12,140
Many of their tests had help:

95
00:04:12,220 --> 00:04:15,020
safety-vest style reflective
material on the target,

96
00:04:15,100 --> 00:04:17,275
which sends far more light straight back.

97
00:04:17,355 --> 00:04:19,920
Our clip's files don't say
if the walker wore any.

98
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:24,480
But in a separate test, the authors report
tracking a person in ordinary clothes,

99
00:04:24,560 --> 00:04:26,745
capturing thirty frames a second.

100
00:04:26,825 --> 00:04:28,585
What might this be good for?

101
00:04:28,665 --> 00:04:31,815
Picture a delivery robot nearing
a blind warehouse corner.

102
00:04:31,895 --> 00:04:33,895
With a suitable wall at the junction,

103
00:04:33,975 --> 00:04:37,580
a sensor like this might give it an
early hint of movement out of sight.

104
00:04:37,660 --> 00:04:41,100
Just a fuzzy blob:
enough to say slow down,

105
00:04:41,180 --> 00:04:42,720
not enough to say who's there.

106
00:04:42,800 --> 00:04:45,920
And plenty is still hard: short range,

107
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:48,880
dark or shiny walls, bright sunlight,

108
00:04:48,960 --> 00:04:51,045
and fast math on small hardware.

109
00:04:51,125 --> 00:04:53,980
The researchers call it
an early-stage prototype.

110
00:04:54,060 --> 00:04:56,380
For now, it's a promising clue,

111
00:04:56,460 --> 00:04:58,160
not a safety system.

112
00:04:58,240 --> 00:05:01,540
Being out of sight isn't the
same as giving nothing away.

113
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:04,880
To really hide, he'd have
to block the bounces too.

114
00:05:07,655 --> 00:05:12,055
This is Future Got Weird: the
strange future, explained.

115
00:05:12,135 --> 00:05:15,215
Subscribe for more, and we'll
see you around the corner.
